{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1498 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a21d3a34e3428618b603a56994146b668f56755b9eff0a4a68dfa07537035f1a" :edb/db-before-t 1497 :edb/db-after-t 1498 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534810 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:43:18.047Z" 13194139534810 true] [17592186115097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311445 13194139534810 true] [17592186115097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311464 13194139534810 true] [17592186115098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311450 13194139534810 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311439 13194139534810 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311457 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311440 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311443 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1089 "q-324396" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1100 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1113 #uuid "e0811f8e-77b1-581c-9a3b-49215eb91c06" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311439 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{78.463}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/e05c5b50dc8308d278cb6dac3408349da1865d37601003e0ee3025c937404239.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{360}^{∘}-{78.463}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{281.537}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=78.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}$\nand $x=281.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311441 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311442 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1041 "field-1" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311442 13194139534810 true] [17592186311440 1110 #uuid "26f0f00d-12e5-5e5c-af9a-a051137faf9b" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311441 1034 "78.4" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311441 1111 #uuid "1881eefe-e262-5134-8f39-9602f168ac73" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311442 1034 "78.5" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311442 1111 #uuid "b10494cc-d252-5978-b1a4-db1487a0a91b" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311443 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311444 13194139534810 true] [17592186311443 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311443 1041 "field-2" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311443 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311444 13194139534810 true] [17592186311443 1110 #uuid "e70b34da-e74d-5abc-9184-813d0d76b95a" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311444 1034 "281.5" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311444 1111 #uuid "c0897369-ac95-5e88-8e19-1efce92a255e" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311446 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311448 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1089 "q-325113" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1100 "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1113 #uuid "f0b4a712-747b-5f24-a07c-8792364dfb5d" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311445 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $x={120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/29560b074afbb7d4e0cde3c1964e14696ef9879618e42c0b0c41f478e7ffcb72.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the third quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-120\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=120\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311446 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311447 13194139534810 true] [17592186311446 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311446 1041 "field-1" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311446 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311447 13194139534810 true] [17592186311446 1110 #uuid "c0006b45-c61a-57d2-bd9d-6f06140121b8" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311447 1034 "-120" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311447 1111 #uuid "7ade66cd-d4ea-52b3-8c97-f074b46d9065" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311448 1041 "field-2" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311449 13194139534810 true] [17592186311448 1110 #uuid "6e8ee893-56a2-5891-b9a4-7a32d4796e8e" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311449 1034 "120" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311449 1111 #uuid "eda27441-a598-55a1-98e8-d0f8dd842437" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311451 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1089 "q-66012" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{4}$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1113 #uuid "fe19d5a7-c609-58e7-8ed1-cb1a6db89bee" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311450 1145 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311452 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311455 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311456 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1041 "selection" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311451 1110 #uuid "293d9783-5238-5551-9dc6-6fa13b640c9a" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311452 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311452 1111 #uuid "9e329fae-76c1-567f-a977-1cf3bb3a985d" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311453 1034 "III only" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311453 1111 #uuid "0709cf0d-44ce-57f0-bd22-3f56c2bde43d" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311454 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311454 1111 #uuid "f11aef9c-4d45-5c24-b2d6-d054fa6edbc9" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311455 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311455 1111 #uuid "1335c7b4-c666-572b-adc8-e0aa103ccd53" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311456 1034 "II only" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534810 true] [17592186311456 1111 #uuid "4412eb27-8cc3-5e2e-a7a2-c161c6ef2ac4" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311458 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1089 "q-76490" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1113 #uuid "7e4cf6db-dd8e-5ff4-9ab3-5bd762661521" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311457 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=1.403\\,348…≈1.403\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.403\\,3$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.403\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/9b3d60be42ec916f0e62f3a86cb1ce2aa628482c97f68f0df7c6ab533b919102.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.403\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.879\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.403\\,3\\,\\cdot 4.879\\,9≈6.8\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311459 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311460 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311461 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311462 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311463 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1041 "selection" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311463 13194139534810 true] [17592186311458 1110 #uuid "4e094223-59b6-58d3-90b2-590de6868b00" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311459 1034 "7.4" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311459 1111 #uuid "ea39e055-5de4-585f-97ed-39e723985c28" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311460 1034 "4.9" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311460 1111 #uuid "f90118d7-f44b-59be-bf1f-2d7b079983d9" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311461 1034 "5.8" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311461 1111 #uuid "18c72fd7-7074-5ae2-9400-b44091936f54" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311462 1034 "6.2" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311462 1111 #uuid "64c8e833-cba1-5130-8af1-abf68cf117a4" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311463 1034 "6.8" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311463 1111 #uuid "27318a66-d3e2-5621-8781-bbe4df52f384" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311465 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1089 "q-76499" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1113 #uuid "4e8521fd-ad2a-559c-8610-d33a7fbf2c30" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311464 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get the first solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/afb20de50d2fa92c6550d1e5357b2aaf07311177e3ed82e1a15facccf2c0f0db.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value does not lie in the required domain. To get the solution that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311466 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311467 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311468 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311469 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186311470 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1041 "selection" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1104 #edb/ref 17592186311467 13194139534810 true] [17592186311465 1110 #uuid "ae8ea27e-a9c0-5393-a13c-254b828bd181" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311466 1034 "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311466 1111 #uuid "2a74faa0-7119-5997-841d-9b5618bdc696" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311467 1034 "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311467 1111 #uuid "ca25e273-1120-52a0-9763-1d7b602fc5ce" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311468 1034 "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" 13194139534810 true] [17592186311468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534810 true] [17592186311468 1111 #uuid "a189b7